# family: clock
# bridge: B(tick-schedule)
# regime: exact (tick-domain integers; continuous readings at 6 digits)
# instrument-first: f467c21db3f9
# baseline: tests/unit/clock
# baseline-count: 19
#
# The unit is the tick engine inside ClockCore, which is now NetwClockCore:
# the schedule, the simulation gate and its step budget, and the calibration.
#
# THE SAME TWO-PLANE SPLIT LIVENESS TOOK, in a different place. ClockCore was
# never one class's worth of concern:
#
#   engine     the tick loop, the gate, the calibration, the RTT window.
#              Arithmetic over integers and durations, no Object, no send.
#   interface  the ping/pong messages (they reach api._replication, GDScript
#              until W4), the MultiplayerClock node, Netw.dbg, and the
#              wall-clock cadence counters.
#
# The ENGINE migrated completely, so the recipe's "a class migrates completely
# or not at all" holds on the class that crossed, and nothing straddles the two
# tiers. What stays in clock_core.gd is the interface, and it dies at W4/W6.
#
# THE NAME. The native class is NetwClockCore and the GDScript interface keeps
# the name ClockCore, which reads oddly beside LivenessShell. Renaming it is a
# 114-reference sweep across 46 files and it is not this batch's job. TRIGGER:
# rename to ClockShell when the interface crosses, where the sweep is already
# being paid.
#
# WHAT FORCED WHAT, swept rather than assumed. Six sites outside clock_core.gd
# reach its privates and only one forces anything:
#   netw_multiplayer.gd    _configured = true          stays a GDScript var
#   replication_core.gd    the four _handle_* receivers  force NOTHING
#   signal_counter.gd      _calibrate, in a doc example  doc drift only
# The four receivers force nothing because they are themselves interface-plane:
# each reaches api._replication or the node, so each STAYS GDScript beside the
# caller that dispatches into it. What they needed instead was one public read
# for the state the port took away, and NetwClockCore.tick_phase() is it.
#
# THE INSTRUMENT is tests/crossing/clock_arm.gd against
# tests/native/goldens/clock_schedules.txt: 71 rows over 13 scenarios, recorded
# at f467c21d before a line of the engine was native, and red-proven three
# times across three independent halves (a quantized server phase, a step
# budget that dropped physics_steps_per_tick, a moved stall threshold). All 71
# rows reproduced unchanged after the port.
#
# The arm reaches ZERO privates, which is why it could be recorded at all
# against a class whose own suites reached five. handle_pong answers the entire
# calibration result as a dictionary, so behaviour was available without
# reaching for state.
#
# TWO READINGS ARE DELIBERATELY UNCOVERED by the golden and owed to the native
# restatement instead: tick_factor and cadence() both read the wall clock, so
# neither is a function of the calls alone and neither belongs in a trace.
#
# S5 AND THE RESET LISTENER, the two other items §5 W2 puts in this bullet.
#
# The RESET LISTENER'S PROMOTION from report-only to failure is REFUSED, and
# the refusal is the measurement. Neither half of the per-case census can carry
# a gate. The OBJECT half is noisy for a known reason: the library build cannot
# flush the delete queue, so five transport rig cases report between +144 and
# +304 objects for nodes they correctly handed to queue_free. The RESOURCE half
# is worse than noisy, it is VACUOUS: resource_count reads
# OBJECT_RESOURCE_COUNT, which counts the resource CACHE rather than live
# instances, and nothing in the native corpus loads a resource by path. A
# deliberately leaked Resource was planted to check this: it moved the object
# count by one and left the resource count at zero. A gate built on that half
# would be green against every implementation including a leaking one, which is
# the same vacuity the clock arm's first phase scenario had. TRIGGER: promote
# when a trustworthy signal exists — a delete-queue flush the library build can
# drive, or a live-instance count that bypasses the resource cache.
#
# S5 (step_ticks as a native primitive, and LockstepStepper's death) is NOT
# taken here and is not blocked by anything this batch did. The stepper's
# inputs are interface-plane: it sets manual_tick on GDScript ClockCore objects
# and polls MultiplayerAPI instances it is handed. Its session is already
# native, so the primitive is feasible, but the sweep is 13 files including
# game_harness.gd and three tests/support/ rigs, and the seam it wants
# (manual_tick, and clocks addressed as NetwClockCore rather than ClockCore)
# reads more naturally once the interface itself crosses. TRIGGER: take it with
# the interface, at W4/W6, where the same sweep is already being paid.
#
# CARRIED DEBT, named with its trigger. handle_pong still answers a fixed-key
# Dictionary, which the debt rule refuses. Its consumer is
# MultiplayerClock.pong_received, a public signal payload games read, so
# converting it is a public surface change rather than a cleanup. It converts
# when that node crosses at W6. The golden pins all nine keys meanwhile, which
# is the same disposition the table batch gave apply_frame.
#
test_clock_simulation_gate.gd:test_an_ungated_clock_simulates_every_frame               -> clock_core_tests.cpp:"[Networked][Clock][Hosted] L5 an ungated clock simulates every frame"
test_clock_simulation_gate.gd:test_a_gated_clock_holds_the_frames_that_run_no_tick      -> clock_core_tests.cpp:"[Networked][Clock][Hosted] L6 a gated clock holds the frames that run no tick"
